  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1038 PM MDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nHOT AND DRY THROUGH THIS WEEKEND:\n\nHigh pressure remains positioned across the west with the center\nof the high sliding over the Four Corners Friday and Saturday.\nThis will allow temperatures to heat up a bit more with lower\nvalleys seeing triple digit heat. The hotter temperatures will\nbe concentrated over the Grand Valley and lower desert valleys\nof east-central and southeast Utah where Heat Advisories have\nbeen issued earlier for both Friday and Saturday. With the\ncenter more overhead, the gradient winds will relax some so even\nthough relative humidity will be very dry in the single digits,\nwinds overall should remain below critical fire weather\nthresholds. Some localized critical fire weather conditions are\npossible at times but not widespread enough to warrant Fire\nWeather highlights. Some moisture does look to work in from the\nwest around the high on Saturday and Sunday, which looks to at\nleast result in a bit of cloud cover. Isolated storms are\npossible over the eastern Uintas and southern San Juans but look\nto produce gusty outflow winds over any wetting rain. Confidence\nis pretty low though at this point as the air mass remains hot\nand dry with little in the way of forcing to sustain any\nconvection if it does develop.\n\nPOTENTIAL MOISTURE RETURN NEXT WEEK:\n\nA few shortwaves are forecast to track over the Northern \nRockies Sunday evening into next week, trying to break down the \nridge in place a bit and in turn allow for moisture to begin \nsneaking in underneath the high. This pattern looks to introduce\nmore cloud cover and potential for afternoon convection mainly \nforming over the high terrain. It may take some time for the \natmosphere to overcome this persistent hot and dry air mass but \nbetter potential for wetting rain (mainly over the higher\nterrain) and more storm coverage is possible late next week as \na trough enters the west coast and nudges the high further east \nto draw up more of a southerly flow. If this comes to fruition, \ntemperatures would cool down a bit with highs closer to normal \nfor this time of year which the current NBM guidance is \nindicating for late next week. Highs are currently around 10 or \nso degrees above normal in this hot, dry pattern so this would \nbe a welcome change.",
